.AB
.PP
Uucp is a series of programs designed to permit communication
between
.UX
systems using either dial-up, hardwired communication or
network communication
lines.
This document gives a detailed implementation
description of the current (second)
implementation of UKUUCP.
.PP
This document is based on the original Implementation Description by
D. A. Nowitz 
and the
.I UKUUCP
changes documented by
L. M. McLoughlin.
.PP
It is for use by an administrator/installer of the system.
It is not meant as a user's guide.
.AE
.CS 12 5 15
.SH
Introduction
.LP
Uucp is a series of programs designed to permit communication between
.UX
systems using either dial-up,
hardwired communication or network communication lines.
It is used for file transfers and remote command execution.
The first versions of the system were designed and implemented
by M. E. Lesk.\s-2\u1\d\s+2
.FS
1 M. E. Lesk and A. S. Cohen,
.UX
Software Distribution by Communication Link,
.ie \n(TN>0 TM-77-1274-3, TM-77-8234-5.
.el private communication.
.FE
Quite extensive corrections and improvements were made by
Tom Truscott, amongst others, from the net.
This paper describes the current (second) implementation
of the UKUUCP system.
.LP
Uucp is a batch type operation.
Files are created in a spool directory for processing
by the uucp demons.
There are three types of files used for the execution
of work.

.I Data\ files
contain data for transfer to remote systems.
.I Work\ files
contain directions for file transfers between systems.
.I Execution\ files
are directions for
.UX
command executions which
involve the resources of one or more systems.
.LP
The uucp system consists of four primary and five
secondary programs.
The primary programs are:
.RS
.IP uucp 10
This program creates work and gathers data files in the spool directory
for the transmission of files.
.IP uux
This program creates work files, execute files and gathers data files for the remote execution of
.UX
commands.
.IP uucico
This program executes the work files for data transmission.
.IP uuxqt
This program executes the execution files for
.UX
command execution.
.RE
.ne 16
.LP
The secondary programs are:
.RS
.IP uuclean 10
This program removes old files from the spool directory.
.IP uulog
This program reports on the status of uucp requests.
.IP uusnap
This program prints a snapshot of the current uucp status.
.IP uustat
This program gathers statistics on uucp requests.
.IP uuname
This program lists known uucp sites with direct connections.
.LP
.RE
The remainder of this paper will describe the operation
of each program, the installation of the system,
the security aspects of the system,
the files required for execution,
and the administration of the system.
.NH
Uucp - UNIX to UNIX File Copy
.LP
The
.I uucp
command is the user's primary interface with the system.
The
.I uucp
command was designed to look like
.I cp
to the user.
The syntax is
.IP
.I uucp\ \ 
.B [
option
.B ]
\ ...\ \ source\ ...\ \ destination
.LP
where the source and destination
may contain the prefix
.I system-name!
which indicates the system on which the file
or files reside
or where they will be copied.
.LP
The options interpreted by
.I uucp
are:
.RS
.IP \-d 10
Make directories when necessary for copying the file.
This is now permanently turned on in UKUUCP, the flag is ignored.
.IP \-c
Don't copy source files to the spool directory,
but use the specified source when the actual
transfer takes place.
.IP \-C
Force making of a copy into the spool directory.
.IP \-g\fIletter\fR
Put
.I letter
in as the grade in the name of the work file.
(This can be used to change the order of work for a particular
machine.)
.IP \-m
Send mail on completion of the work.
.IP \-n\fIname\fR
Send mail to
.I name
on destination system on completion of the work.
.IP \-e\fIname\fR
Run the uucp command on this system rather than locally.
.LP
The following options are used primarily for debugging:
.IP \-r 10
Queue the job but do not start
.I uucico
program.
.IP \-s\fIdir\fR
Use directory
.I dir
for the spool directory.
.IP \-x\fInum\fR
.I Num
is the level of debugging output desired.
.RE
.LP
The destination may be a directory name,
in which case the file name is taken from the last part of the
source's name.
The source
name may contain special shell characters
such as ``\fI?*[]\fR''.
If a source argument has a
.I system-name!
prefix for a remote system,
the file name expansion will be done on the remote system.
.LP
The command
.IP "" 12
uucp\ \ *.c\ \ usg!/usr/dan
.LP
will set up the transfer of all files whose names end with ``.c''
to the ``/usr/dan'' directory on the``usg'' machine.
.LP
The source and/or destination names may also contain a
.I ~user
prefix.
This translates to the login directory on
the specified system.
For names with partial path-names,
the current directory is prepended to the file name.
File names with
.I ../
are not permitted.
.LP
The command
.IP "" 12
uucp\ \ usg!~dan/*.h\ \ ~dan
.LP
will set up the transfer of files whose names end with ``.h''
in dan's login
directory on system ``usg'' to dan's local
login directory.
.LP
If the destination name ends in a 
.I /
then it will be presume it is a directory and create it (if necessary)
to copy files into.
.LP
For each source file,
the program will check the source and destination
file-names
and the system-part of each to
classify the work into one of five types:
.RS
.IP [1]
Copy source to destination on local system.
.IP [2]
Receive files from other systems.
.IP [3]
Send files to a remote systems.
.IP [4]
Send files from remote systems
to another remote system.
.IP [5]
Receive files from remote systems when the source
contains special shell characters as
mentioned above.
.RE
.LP
After the work has been set up in the spool directory,
the
.I uucico
program is started to try to contact the other
machine to execute the work (unless the \-r option
was specified).
.SH
Type 1
.LP
A
.I cp
command is used to do the work.
The
.I \-d
and the
.I \-m
options are not honored in this case.
.SH
Type 2
.LP
A one line
.I "work file"
is created for each file requested and put in the spool directory
with the following fields, each separated by a blank.
(All
.I "work files"
and
.I "execute files"
use a blank as the field separator.)
.RS
.IP [1]
R
.IP [2]
The full path-name of the source or a ~user/path-name.
The
.I ~user
part will be expanded on the remote system.
.IP [3]
The full path-name of the destination file.
If the
.I ~user
notation is used, it will be immediately
expanded to be the login directory for the user.
.IP [4]
The user's login name.
.IP [5]
A ``\-'' followed by an option list.
.RE
.KS
.SH
Type 3
.LP
For each source file, a
.I "work file"
is created and the source file is copied into a
.I "data file"
in the spool directory.
(A ``\-c'' option on the
.I uucp
command will prevent the
.I "data file"
from being made.)
In this case, the file will be transmitted from
the indicated source.)
The fields of each entry are given below.
.RS
.IP [1]
S
.IP [2]
The full-path name of the source file.
.IP [3]
The full-path name of the destination or
~user/file-name.
.IP [4]
The user's login name.
.IP [5]
A ``\-'' followed by an option list.
.IP [6]
The name of the
.I "data file"
in the spool directory.
.IP [7]
The file mode bits of the source file
in octal print format
(e.g. 0666).
.IP [8]
Name to send mail to on remote system on receival.
.RE
.KE
.SH
Type 4 and Type 5
.LP
.I Uucp
generates a
.I uucp
command and sends it to the remote machine;
the remote
.I uucico
executes the
.I uucp
command.
.SH
Other Types
.LP
If the system was compiled with the ZAP option (see below) then when
a command has been successfully completed it will have its command
character converted to lower case.
.NH
Uux - UNIX To UNIX Execution
.LP
The
.I uux
command is used to set up the execution of a
.UX
command
where the execution machine and/or some of the
files are remote.
The syntax of the uux command is
.IP
.I uux\ \ 
.B [
\-
.B "] ["
option
.B ]
\ ...\ \ command-string
.LP
where the command-string is made up of one or more arguments.
All special shell characters such as ``<>|^'' must be quoted
either by quoting the entire command-string
or quoting the character as a separate argument.
Within the command-string, the command and file names may
contain a
.I system-name!
prefix.
All arguments which do not contain a ``!'' will not
be treated as files.
(They will not be copied to the execution machine.)
The ``\-'' is used to indicate that the standard input
for
.I command-string
should be inherited from the standard input
of the
.I uux
command.
The options:
.RS
.IP \-r 10
Don't start
.I uucico
or
.I uuxqt
after queuing the job;
.IP \-x\fInum\fR
Num is the level of debugging output desired.
.IP \-n
Don't return notification on completion.
.IP \-z
Don't return notification unless command fails.
.IP \-gX
Queue the work at the grade given by the character X, 'A' is highest
.IP \-OX
If a uucico is spawned restrict it to doing work only for grade X.
.RE
.LP
The command
.IP "" 12
pr\ \ abc\ \ |\ \ uux\ \ \-\ \ usg!lpr
.LP
will set up the output of ``pr abc''
as standard input to an lpr command
to be executed on system ``usg''.
.LP
.I Uux
generates an
.I "execute file"
which contains the
names of the files required
for execution (including standard input),
the user's login name, the destination
of the standard output, and the command to be executed.
This file is either put in the spool directory
for local execution or sent to the remote system using
a generated send command (type 3 above).
.LP
For required files which are not on the execution machine,
.I uux
will generate receive command files (type 2 above).
These command-files will be put on the execution machine and
executed by the
.I uucico
program.
(This will work only if the local system has permission
to put files in the remote spool directory as controlled
by the remote
.I USERFILE .
)
.LP
The
.I "execute file"
will be processed
by the
.I uuxqt
program on the execution machine.
It is made up of several lines,
each of which contains an identification character
and one or more arguments.
The order of the lines in the file is not relevant
and some of the lines may not be present.
Each line is described below.
.RS
.SH
User Line
.IP
U\ \ user\ \ system
.LP
where the
.I user
and
.I system
are the requester's login name and system.
.SH
Required File Line
.IP
F file-name real-name
.LP
where the
.I file-name
is the generated name of a file for the execute machine
and
.I real-name
is the last part of the actual file name (contains no
path information).
Zero or more of these lines may be present in the
.I "execute file" .
The
.I uuxqt
program will check for the existence of all required
files before the command is executed.
.SH
Standard Input Line
.IP
I\ \ file-name
.LP
The standard input is either specified by a ``<'' in the
command-string or inherited from the standard input of the
.I uux
command if the ``\-'' option is used.
If a standard input is not specified,
``/dev/null'' is used.
.SH
Standard Output Line
.IP
O\ \ file-name\ \ system-name
.LP
The standard output is specified by a ``>'' within the
command-string.
If a standard output is not specified,
``/dev/null'' is used.
(Note \- the use of ``>>'' is not implemented.)
.SH
Command Line
.IP
C\ \ command\ \ 
.B [
arguments
.B ]
\ ...
.LP
The arguments are those specified in the command-string.
The standard input and standard output will not appear on this
line.
All
.I "required files"
will be moved to the execution directory (a subdirectory
of the spool directory)
and the
.UX
command is executed using the Shell specified in the
.I uucp.h
header file.
In addition, a shell ``PATH'' statement is prepended
to the command line as specified in the
.I L.cmds
file (see uuxqt).
.LP
After execution, the standard output is copied or set up to be
sent to the proper place.
.SH
Notification Command Lines.
.LP
There are two command lines to govern when notification of command
command completion should be mailed back to the originator. 
The
.I Z
command corresponds to the 
.I \-z
option to 
.I uux
and will cause notification to be sent if the execution of the program
generates a non-zero exit status.
The 
.I N
command corresponds to the 
.I \-n
option to 
.I uux
and will disable the sending of notification except for failed rmail
commands.
.RE
.NH
Uucico - Copy In, Copy Out
.LP
The
.I uucico
program will perform the following major functions:
.RS
.IP -\ \  3
Scan the spool directory for work.
.IP -\ \  
Place a call to a remote system.
.IP -\ \ 
Negotiate a line protocol to be used.
.IP -\ \ 
Execute all requests from both systems.
.IP -\ \ 
Log work requests and work completions.
.RE
.LP
.I Uucico
may be started in several ways;
.RS
.IP a) 5
by a system daemon,
.IP b)
by one of the
.I
uucp, uux, uuxqt
.R
or
.I uucico
programs,
.IP c)
directly by the user (this is usually for testing),
.IP d)
by a remote system.
(The uucico program should be specified as the ``shell''
field in the ``/etc/passwd'' file for the ``uucp'' logins.)
.LP
NOTE:  Only sites listed in the L.sys file will be allowed to invoke
uucico as a slave.  All other incoming callers will be rejected.
.RE
.LP
When started by method a, b or c, the program is considered to
be in
.I MASTER
mode.
In this mode, a connection will be made to a remote system.
If started by a remote system (method d),
the program is considered to be in
.I SLAVE
mode.
.LP
The
.I MASTER
mode will operate in one of two ways.
If no system name is specified
(\-s option not specified)
the program will scan the spool directory for
systems to call.
If a system name is specified, that system will be called,
and work will only be done for that system.
.LP
The
.I uucico
program is generally started by another program.
There are several options used for execution:
.RS
.IP \-r1 10
Start the program in
.I MASTER
mode.
This is used when
.I uucico
is started by a program or ``cron'' shell.
.IP \-s\fIsys\fR
Do work only for system
.I sys.
If
.I \-s
is specified,
a call to the specified system
will be made even if there is no work for system
.I sys
in the spool directory.
This is useful for polling systems which do not have
the hardware to initiate a connection.
.IP \-Y
After connection flip over roles.  So when the site is called it is
allowed to send out its mail (and perform other transfers) first. This is
for connections with a small ammount of outgoing traffic (eg: mail) but a
large ammount of incoming traffic (eg: news).
.IP \-O\fIgrade\fR
Do work only for commands with the given
.I grade.
.IP \-W\fIsize\fR
Change the window size used by the transfer protocol to be
.I size.
This is usually used in conjunction with the following flag, and the
comment applied to it applies to this option as well.
.IP \-p\fIsize\fR
Change the packet size used by the transfer protocol to be 
.I size.
Only if running with the 
.I DATAWIDTH
code.  It is esential for tuning the connection when communicating over a
network.
.IP \-t
Ignore the times-to-call field of the
.I L.sys
file.  This is to force all work to be spooled.  Have no legal times-to-call
and all work will be queued then periodically connect to the remote system
by specifying
.I \-t.
.IP \-H\fIsysname\fR
When connecting to the remote host claim to be the system
.I sysname.
This is to allow multi-machine site to appear as one host to the outside
world.  This can only be used by authorised users and even they should
not use it unless they are very sure of its usage!  (See also the L.info
file below.)
.LP
The following options are used primarily for debugging:
.IP \-d\fIdir\fR
Use directory
.I dir
for the spool directory.
.IP \-x\fInum\fR
.I Num
is the level of debugging output desired.
.RE
.LP
The next part of this section will describe the major steps within
the
.I uucico
program.
.SH
Scan For Work
.LP
The names of the work related files in the spool directory have format
.IP
type . system-name grade number
.LP
where:
.IP
.I Type
is an upper case letter,
(
.I C
-\ copy command file,
.I D
-\ data file,
.I X
-\ execute file);
.IP
.I System-name
is the remote system;
.IP
.I Grade
is a character;
.IP
.I Number
is a four charcter, padded sequence number.
.LP
The file
.IP "" 12
C.res45n0031
.LP
would be a
.I "work file"
for a file transfer between the local
machine and the ``res45'' machine.
.LP
The scan for work is done by looking through the
spool directory for
.I "work files"
(files with prefix ``C.'').
When you have sub-directories all subdirectories whose name begins with
the correct suffix are scanned.
A list is made of all systems to be called.
.I Uucico
will then call each system and process all
.I "work files" .
.SH
Call Remote System
.LP
The call is made using information from several
files which reside in the uucp program directory.
At the start of the call process, a lock is
set to forbid multiple conversations 
between the same two systems.
.LP
The system name is found in the
.I L.sys
file.
The information contained for each system is;
.RS
.IP [1]
system name,
.IP [2]
times to call the system
(days-of-week and times-of-day),
.IP [3]
device or device type to be used for call,
.IP [4]
line speed,
.IP [5]
datawidth.  Normally
.I 8fN
for modem or direct lines but is set (for example to 
.I 7fN)
when communicating over a network or 
.I 8fY
if the fio protocol is to be used.
.IP [6]
phone number if field [3] is
.I ACU
or the device name (same as field [3])
if not
.I ACU,
.IP [7]
login information (multiple fields),
.RE
.LP
The time field is checked against the present time to see
if the call should be made.
.LP
The
.I
phone number
.R
may contain abbreviations (e.g. mh, py, boston) which get translated into dial
sequences using the
.I L-dialcodes
file.
.LP
The
.I L-devices
file is scanned using fields [3] and [4] (and for direct lines [6]) from the
.I L.sys
file to find an available device for the call.
The program will try all devices which satisfy
[3] and [4] until the call is made, or no more
devices can be tried.
If a device is successfully opened, a lock file
is created so that another copy of
.I uucico
will not try to use it.
For a direct device type (field [3] is 
.I DIR)
fields [4] and [6] have to match with the corresponding entries for the
device to be selected.
If the call is complete, the
.I
login information
.R
(field [6] and onwards of
.I L.sys )
is used to login.
.LP
The conversation between the two
.I uucico
programs begins with a handshake started by the called,
.I SLAVE ,
system.
The
.I SLAVE
sends a message to let the
.I MASTER
know it is ready to receive the system
identification and conversation sequence number.
The response from the
.I MASTER
is
verified by the
.I SLAVE
and if acceptable, protocol selection begins.
The
.I SLAVE
can also reply with a ``call-back required''
message in which case, the current conversation
is terminated.
.SH
Line Protocol Selection
.LP
The remote system sends a message
.IP "" 12
P\fIproto-list\fR
.LP
where proto-list is a string of characters, each
representing a line protocol.
.LP
The calling program checks the proto-list
for a letter corresponding to an available line
protocol and returns a
.I use-protocol
message.
The
.I use-protocol
message is
.IP "" 12
U\fIcode\fR
.LP
where code is either a one character
protocol letter or
.I N
which means there is no common protocol.
.SH
Work Processing
.LP
The initial roles (
.I MASTER
or
.I SLAVE
) for the work processing are
the mode in which each program starts.
(The 
.I MASTER
has been specified by the ``\-r1'' uucico option.)
The
.I MASTER
program does a work search similar to the
one used in the ``Scan For Work'' section.
.LP
There are five messages used during the
work processing, each specified by the first
character of the message.
They are;
.IP "" 12
.RS
.IP S 3
send a file,
.IP R
receive a file,
.IP C
copy complete,
.IP X
execute a
.I uucp
command,
.IP H
hangup.
.RE
.LP
The
.I MASTER
will send
.I R ,
.I S
or
.I X
messages until all work from the spool directory is
complete, at which point an
.I H
message will be sent.
The
.I SLAVE
will reply with
\fISY\fR, \fISN\fR, \fIRY\fR, \fIRN\fR, \fIHY\fR, \fIHN\fR,
\fIXY\fR, \fIXN\fr,
corresponding to
.I yes
or
.I no
for each request.
.LP
The send and receive replies are
based on permission to access the
requested file/directory using the
.I USERFILE
and read/write permissions of the file/directory.
After each file is copied into the spool directory
of the receiving system,
a copy-complete message is sent by the receiver of the file.
The message
.I CY
will be sent if the
file has successfully been moved from the
temporary spool file to the actual destination.
Otherwise, a
.I CN
message is sent.
(In the case of
.I CN ,
the transferred file will be in the spool
directory with a name beginning with ``TM'.)
The requests and results are logged on both systems.
.LP
The hangup response is determined by the
.I SLAVE
program by a work scan of the spool directory.
If work for the remote system exists in the
.I SLAVE's
spool directory, an
.I HN
message is sent and the programs switch roles.
If no work exists, an
.I HY
response is sent.
.SH
Conversation Termination
.LP
When a
.I HY
message is received by the
.I MASTER
it is echoed back to the
.I SLAVE
and the protocols are turned off.
Each program sends a final ``OO'' message to the
other.
The original
.I SLAVE
program will clean up and terminate.
The
.I MASTER
will proceed to call other systems
and process work as long as possible
or terminate if a
.I \-s
option was specified.
.LP
.NH
Uuxqt - Uucp Command Execution
.LP
The
.I uuxqt
program is used to execute
.I
execute files
.R
generated by
.I uux.
The
.I uuxqt
program may be started by either the
.I uucico
or
.I uux
programs.
The program scans the spool directory for
.I
execute files
.R
(prefix ``X.'').
Each one is checked to see if all the required files are available and
if so, the command line or send line is executed.
.LP
The
.I
execute file
.R
is described in the ``Uux''
section above.
.SH
Command Execution
.LP
The execution is accomplished by executing a
.I
sh \-c
.R
of the command line after appropriate
standard input and standard output have been opened.
If a standard output is specified, the program
will create a send command or copy the output
file as appropriate.
.NH
Uuclean - Uucp Spool Directory Cleanup
.LP
This program is typically started by the daemon, once a day.
Its function is to remove files from the spool directory which
are more than 3 days old.
These are usually files for work which can not be completed.
.LP
.LP
The options available are:
.RS
.IP \-d\fIdir\fR 10
The directory to be scanned is
.I dir .
.IP \-m
Send mail to the owner of each file being removed.
(Note that most files put into the spool directory
will be owned by the owner of the
uucp programs since the setuid bit will be set on these
programs.
The mail will therefore most often go to the owner
of the uucp programs.)
.IP \-n\fIhours\fR
Change the aging time from 72 hours to
.I hours
hours.
.IP \-p\fIpre\fR
Examine files with prefix
.I pre
for deletion.
(Up to 10 file prefixes may be specified.)
.IP \-x\fInum\fR
This is the level of debugging output desired.
.IP \-A
Only when running with subdirectories (eg: the standard UKUUCP version).
Read the
.I L.dirs
file and scan each directory listed for all files in that
directory whose prefix is the directory name.  For the aging time use the
expirey field associated with the directory name in
.I L.dirs.
.NH
Uulog - Uucp Log Inquiry
.LP
Interrogate the uucp log of conversations either completed on in
progress.
The output request is specified by the use of the
following options:
.RS
.IP \-s\fIsys\fR 9
Print entries where
.I sys
is the remote system name;
.IP \-u\fIuser\fR
Print entries for user
.I user.
.RE
.LP
The intersection of lines satisfying the two options is output.
A null
.I sys
or
.I user
means all system names or users respectively.
.RE
.NH
Uusnap - Uucp Snapshot
.LP
Print a snapshot of the current uucp status.
For all systems which have files present in the spool directory print a
single line of status information.  The sitename, the number of outgoing
commands to be processed, the number of outgoing data files waiting for
transfer, the number of incoming commands waiting to be executed and the
current status of communication with the system.  The status may show that the
system is locked, or that login is failed for example.  If a status field is
printed it will also say wether retry time has been reached.
.NH
Uustat - Uucp Statistics
.LP
Print detailed statistics about all known systems and users.
.RS
.IP \-b 10
print seperate statistics on incoming and outgoing traffic
.IP \-u
print statistics on all known systems and users
.IP \-a
turn on both the other flags.
.RE
.LP
Very detailed statistics are printed on all completed transfers.
.NH
Uuname
.LP
Print a line of known sites to which a direct link
is maintained and the speed of connection to them.
.RS
.IP \-l 10
Just print out the name of the local machine.
.RE
.NH
Security
.LP
.LG
\fB
The uucp system, left unrestricted,
will let any outside user execute any commands
and copy in/out any file which is readable/writable
by the uucp login user.
It is up to the individual sites to be aware of this and
apply the protections that they feel are necessary.
\fR
.NL
.LP
There are several security features available aside from the
normal file mode protections.
These must be set up by the installer of the
.I uucp
system.
.IP - 3
The login for uucp does not get a standard shell.
Instead, the
.I uucico
program is started.
Therefore, the only work that can be done is through
.I uucico .
.IP -
A path check is done on file names that are to be sent
or received.
The
.I USERFILE
supplies the information for these checks.
The
.I USERFILE
can also be set up to require call-back
for certain login-ids.
(See the ``Files required for execution'' section
for the file description.)
.IP -
A conversation sequence count can be set up so
that the called system
can be more confident that the caller
is who he says he is.
.IP -
The
.I uuxqt
reads a list of commands that it
will execute
from the
.I L.cmds
file.
A ``PATH'' shell statement is prepended to the command
line as specifed in the
.I L.cmds
file.
The installer may modify the list or remove the
restrictions as desired.
.IP -
The
.I L.sys
file should be owned by uucp and have mode 0400
to protect the phone numbers and login information
for remote sites.
(Programs uucp, uucico, uux, uuxqt should be also
owned by uucp and have the setuid bit set.)
.NH
Uucp Installation
.LP
There are several source modifications that may be required
before the system programs are compiled.
These relate to the directories used during compilation,
the directories used during execution,
and the local
.I
uucp system-name.
.R
.LP
The four directories are:
.RS
.IP lib 12
(/usr/src/cmd/UKUUCP/uucp)
This directory contains the source files for generating the
.I uucp
system.
.IP program
(/usr/lib/uucp)
This is the directory used for the executable system programs and
the system files.
.IP spool
(/usr/spool/uucp)
This is the spool directory used during
.I uucp
execution.
.IP xqtdir
(/usr/spool/uucp/XTMP)
This directory is used during execution of
.I "execute files" .
.RE
.LP
The names given in parentheses above are the default values
for the directories.
The italicized named
.I
lib, program, xqtdir,
.R
and
.I spool
will be used in the following text to represent the
appropriate directory names.
.LP
There are two files which may require modification,
the
.I makefile
file and the
.I uucp.h
file.
The following paragraphs describe the modifications.
The modes of
.I spool
and
.I xqtdir
should be made ``0777''.
.SH
Uucp.h modification
.LP
Starting from the top of the file.
.RS
.IP Sitename\ selection. 10
Select the appropriate method for finding the site name, depending on your
particular version of
.UX
and possibly depending on any existing mail packages.  If the comments in
.I uucp.h
are not explicit enough then look at the file
.I uucpname.c.
.IP MYNAME
Change this define 
to be the current system name as a fail-safe mechanism.  If you are
confident that the mechanism chosen to find the sitename will always work then
you may wish to change the sitename to be some obviously incorrect string.
If it ever comes up then at least it will be noticed.  (See also
L.info below.)
.IP ZAP
When a file has been successfully sent mark the command to prevent
retransmission.  This should only be used if the stdio library on your
system implements fseek on read/write files properly.  To check this
.DS L
	cc tseek.c;a.out
.DE
and if it suggests that all is OK then run with ZAP.  Without ZAP the system

.IP ACCT
Define to enable full accounting of all calls and of all files transfered
or jobs run.
.IP KICKINIT
If you wish to use lines for both login and dialout
.I AND
you have a program capable of performing the necessary work (see in
.I UUAIDS
for examples) then define this.
.IP WS
Define if you are using Bill Sebok's
.I acu
program to do the 
.I KICKINIT
work.
.IP DATAWIDTH
Define this to enable uucico to work over communications lines that do not
provide full eight bit transparent connections, such as X3/X25 and PSS.
.IP FOURBIT
This is a version of the narrow datawidth code that is the most immune to
network pecularities.  It is also the slowest.
.IP SEVENBIT
This is another version of the narrow datawidth code.  It presumes a reasonable
network and provides a respectably high transput.  (Note that
.I SIXBIT
is very similar to this.)
.IP LIBNDIR
When this is defined then the directory scanning routines will be taken from
the
.I LIBNDIR
subdirectory.
.IP NDIR
In this case the directory scanning routines will be taken from
.I /usr/lib/libndir.a
and the includes from
.I /usr/include/ndir.h.
.IP BSD42
In this case the directory scanning routines are presumed to be part of the
the standard library.
.IP SYSIII
define this if you are running on a system 3 compatiable system.
.IP SYS5
define this if you are running on a system 5 compatiable system.
.IP BAD_PRINTF
on some systems a printf format of "%02d" doesn't print out
the argument in two characters with leading zeros
.DS L
cc prtest.c;a.out
.DE
to find out if you're machine is one. In this case define BAD_PRINTF to
have it use the "%0.2d" format instead.
.IP OLDTOUCH
define this if your chmod() system call modifies the ctime field.
.IP WFMASK
The value of this mask is used to ommit permissions on uucp work files
.IP LOGMASK
This is the umask for all LOGFILEs.
.IP BASEMODE
final mode given to files at the remote site.  Remember that they should
at least be readable.
.IP LOCKDIR
This is the directory where lock files will be created.  It must
be writeably by uucp.  Normally this is UUCPDIR/uucp but many sites
prefer to change it to be "/tmp so that it will be automatically
cleaned on system restart.  If the ucu program is to be used then
"/tmp is a better choice since ordinary users will not have to be
given write permission on the main uucp directory.
.IP PRIV_UIDS
users with a getuid() <= PRIV_UIDS are 'priviledged' and allowed to run
restricted options (such as tracing on remote login).  (Not used on
sites running SYSINFO.)
.IP UUCPPEOPLE
these are people who can run priviledged by whose uid are not in the range
or 
.I PRIV_UIDS
(not used on sites running SYSINFO).
.IP UUCPDIR
this is the parent directory for 
.I spool
and
the
.I uucp
public directory.
.IP Logfiles
.I LOGFILE
and
.I ERRLOG
are normally kept in the 
.I spool
directory, if
.I UKC
is defined (see Makefile Modification) then they are kept in /usr/adm/log.
Warning: these files can get very large.
.IP MAIL
the mailer used for sending notifications.
.RE
.SH
makefile modification
.LP
There are several
.I make
variable definitions which may need modification.
.RS
.IP DESTDIR 10
The entire
.I UUCP
system is installed below here.  If you modify this you will have to globally
edit all strings '"/' and insert the string you are using for destdir before
the '/'.
.IP SRCDIR
Where the sources for the system reside.
.IP OWNER
Who the owner of the uucp system will be.  Normally a new user
.I \"uucp\"
is created for this.
.IP GROUP
Which group will own the uucp system. Normally a new group 
.I \"uucp\"
is created for this.
.IP CHOWN
the path name of the chown program so that it can be used by root.
.IP CHGRP
the path name of the chgrp program so that it can be used by root.
.IP RANLIB
the path name of the ranlib program.  If ranlib is not present on your
system set this to "echo".
.IP INST
the name of the program used to install software.  Often there will be
a /usr/bin/install if this is not present on you system use the
.I inst.sh
script provided.
.IP MYNAME
The name of the local site.  This is used when subdirectories are present
to create the initial set of subdirectories and to fill in the 
.I L.dirs
file correctly.
.IP Directories\ 
.I LIBDEPS,
.I LIBNDIR,
and
.I LIBS,
If you defined LIBNDIR in
.I uucp.h
then use the first set of definitions supplied.
If NDIR was defined then
use the second set of definitions.
If BSD42 then use the third set.
.IP UUCPDIR
The parent directory for
.I spool
and for the public directory.  Often this is set to be whichever filestore
has the most free space.
It should be set to the same value as the uucp.h version.
.IP LOCN
This is the location of the system.  It is actually 
used to select the SCCS ID used.
.RS
.IP NOSCCS 10
removes all sccs strings.
.IP ORIGSCCS
use the now totally out of date original sccs strings.
.IP UKC
used with
.I UKC .
.IP BIGLDFLAGS\ 
give whichever loading flags are most suitable for large programs.
.RE
.IP FIO
Defining FIO will allow the use of the 'f' protocol by Piet Beertema.
This protocol will only work on connections which have flow-control
working correctly.  JNT-PAD's seem to fail.  (DATAWIDTH must be defined
so FIO can be disabled for a single line by appending 'fN' to the
end of the width entry.)  With FIO defined you should also add
.DS
	-Dvoid=int
.DE
for systems without the void cast. (Check first to see if none of
the include file in your system have a similar #define or
typedef already.)   On Vaxen also add
.DS
	-Dvax
.DE
if not already present,  this is to get the correct version of the
checksum routines.  
FIO will never be selected
for non direct lines.
.SH
Compile the system
.LP
.I
BEFORE DOING GOING FUTHER READ THE INSTALL_GUIDE SINCE IT IS (UNFORTUNELY)
NORMALLY MORE UP TO DATE.
.R
.LP
The command
.IP "" 12
rebuild.sh
.LP
will rebuild and recompile the entire system.
The command
.IP "" 12
make mkdirs
will create the initial set of directories and fill in the
.I L.dirs
file.
.IP "" 12
make install
.LP
will copy the commands to the
to the appropriate directories.
.LP
The programs
.I uucp ,
.I uux ,
.I uusnap ,
.I uustat ,
.I uuname ,
.I uulog ,
.I 
and
.I uulog
should be put in ``/usr/bin''.
The programs
.I uuxqt ,
.I uucico ,
and
.I uuclean
should be put in the
.I program
directory.
.SH
Files required for execution
.LP
There are seven (eight if sharing lines for login and call out)
files which are required for execution,
all of which should reside in the
.I program
directory.
.LP
Unless otherwise stated space or tab can act as a field seperator.
A line may be continued by putting a backslash at the end of the line
in which case the following line is concatenated on and the backslash 
removed.  So long as the total number of characters that the are in the
final line does not exceed (about) a thousand any number of lines may
be joined together in this way.
Any line (except a continuation line) that begins with a hash
('#') symbol is ignored.  This is used for comments.
.SH
L-devices
.LP
This file contains entries for the call-unit devices and
hardwired connections which are to be used by
.I uucp.
The special device files are assumed to be in the
.I /dev
directory.
The format for each entry is
.IP "" 12
caller\ \ line\ \ usefull\ \ class\ \ brand
.LP
where;
.RS
.IP caller 12
is the type of device that is on the line (e.g. ACU or DIR for
auto-dialers and direct lines respectively)
.IP line
is the device for the line (e.g. cul0 or tty68),
.IP usefull
for an ACU this is occasionally
the automatic call unit associated with
.I line
(e.g. cua0),
most modern auto-dialers (and no direct lines)
require an extra line so this field is
normally set to "unused"
.IP class
this has at least a leading number which is used for the line speed
and often contains a trailing word (depending on the device).
.IP brand
This is the ``brand'' of ACU or DIR.  For example "msystem", "racaln",
"racalo" for ACU's or "pad", "ethernet" or "camring" for DIR's.  In the
case of a direct line (DIR) this brand will be matched against the phone-number
field so that particular groups of lines may be selected.
.RE
.LP
The line
.IP "" 12
ACU\ \ cul0\ \ cua0\ \ 300\ \ dn11
.LP
would be set up for a system which
had device cul0 wired to a call-unit
cua0 for use at 300 baud.
.IP "" 12
DIR\ \ tty05\ \ unused\ \ 9600\ \ pad
.IP "" 12
DIR\ \ tty06\ \ unused\ \ 9600\ \ pad
would be set up for a system which had a direct line attached to tty05
to run at 9600 baud and a similiar line attached to tty06.  If an L.sys
entry now required a DIR line at 9600 Baud and the phone-number field
was "pad" it would first try tty05 if it was busy then it would try tty06.
.SH
L-dialcodes
.LP
This file contains entries with location abbreviations used
in the
.I L.sys
file (e.g. london, kent).
The entry format is
.IP "" 12
abb\ \ dial-seq
.LP
where;
.RS
.IP abb 12
is the abbreviation,
.IP dial-seq
is the dial sequence to call that location.
.RE
.LP
The line
.IP "" 12
london\ \ 01
.LP
would be set up so that a phone-number entry of london1247654 would
send 0112347654 to the dial-unit.
.SH
LOGIN/SYSTEM NAMES
.LP
It is assumed that the
.I "login name"
used by a remote computer to call into a local computer
is not the same as the login name of a normal user
of that local machine.
However, several remote computers may employ the same
login name.
.LP
Each computer is given a unique
.I "system name"
which is transmitted at the start of each call.
This name identifies the calling machine to the called machine.
.SH
USERFILE
.LP
This file contains user accessibility information.
It specifies four types of constraint;
.RS
.IP [1]
which files can be accessed by a normal user of the local machine,
.IP [2]
which files can be accessed from a remote computer,
.IP [3]
which login name is used by a particular remote computer,
.IP [4]
whether a remote computer should be called back in order to confirm
its identity.
.RE
.LP
Each line in the file has the following format
.IP "" 6
login,sys\ \ 
.B [
c
.B ]
\ \ path-name\ \ 
.B [
path-name
.B ]
\ ...
.LP
where;
.RS
.IP login 12
is the login name for a user or the remote computer,
.IP  sys
is the system name for a remote computer,
.IP c
is the optional
.I
call-back required
.R
flag,
.IP path-name
is a path-name prefix that is acceptable for
.I user.
.LP
.RE
.LP
The constraints are implemented as follows.
.RS
.IP [1]
When the program is obeying a command stored on the local machine,
.I MASTER
mode,
the path-names allowed are those given for the first line in the
.I USERFILE
that has a login name that matches the login name of the user
who entered the command.
If no such line is found, the first line with a
.I null
login name is used.
.IP [2]
When the program is responding to a command from a remote machine,
.I SLAVE
mode, the path-names allowed are those given for the first line
in the file that has the system name that matches the system name
of the remote machine.
If no such line is found, the first one with a
.I null
system name is used.
.IP [3]
When a remote computer logs in, the login name that
it uses must appear in the
.I USERFILE .
There may be several lines with the same login name but one of them must
either have the name of the remote system or must contain a
.I null
system name.
.IP [4]
If the line matched in ([3]) contains a ``c'',
the remote machine is called back before any transactions take place.
.RE
.LP
The line
.IP "" 12
u,m  /usr/xyz
.LP
allows machine
.I m
to login with name
.I u
and request the transfer of files whose names start with
``/usr/xyz''.
.LP
The line
.IP "" 12
dan,  /usr/dan
.LP
allows the ordinary user
.I dan
to issue commands for files whose name starts with
``/usr/dan''.
.LP
The lines
.IP "" 12
u,m /usr/xyz  /usr/spool
.br
u,  /usr/spool
.LP
allows any remote machine to login with name
.I u ,
but if its system name is not
.I m ,
it can only ask to transfer files whose names start with
``/usr/spool''.
.LP
The lines
.IP "" 12
root,  /
.br
,   /usr
.LP
allows any user to transfer files beginning with ``/usr''
but the user with login
.I root
can transfer any file.
.SH
L.sys
.LP
Each entry in this file represents one system
which can be called by the local uucp programs.
Each entry has, at least, seven fields:
.IP "" 12
site\ time\ caller\ speed\ width\ phone\ login
.LP
These are described below.
.RS
.SH
site
.LP
The name of the remote system.
.SH
time
.LP
This is a string which indicates the days-of-week
and times-of-day when the system should
be called
(e.g. MoTuTh0800\-1730).
.LP
The day portion may be a list containing
some of
.IP
.I
Su Mo Tu We Th Fr Sa
.R
.LP
or it may be
.I Wk
for any week-day or
.I Any
for any day.
.LP
The time should be a range of times (e.g. 0800\-1230).
If no time portion is specified, any time
of day is assumed to be ok for the call.
.LP
Several possible choices may be given by seperating them with a '|'
(e.g. Mo0800-1730|Tu0000-0600).
.SH
caller
.LP
This is normally either
.I ACU
or
.I DIR
but other type are availble for specialised networks.
.SH
speed
.LP
This is the line speed for the call (e.g. 300).
.SH
width
.LP
This is effectively the number of bits that the transfer protocol will use.
Normally it it 
.I 8fN
for ACU's and most DIR's.  However if the line
is attached to a network that does not provide full eight bit data transfers
(such as PSS or JANET, depending on your connection) then this can be set
to
.I
7 6 4
.R
Where
.I 7
will work reasonably efficiently over most most networks that can provide
interactive terminal work.
.I 4
is for relatively brain-damaged networks and although it can transfer in
the face of great adversity it does so very slowly.
.I 6
is a variant of
.I 7 .
All must have the trailing 
.I fN .
.LP
Over some networks the default packet and window sizes selected by the
.I uucico
packet driver may not be optimal.  These can be changed by adding colon
seperated fields to the end of the width field.  For example
.I 7:2:128
will select a data width of
.I 7 ,
a window size of
.I 2
windows and a packet size of
.I 128
characters.  The defaults for these are a packet size of sixty-four characters
and a window size of three.
.LP
It has been found that for PSS and JANET setting the width field to
.I 7:2
(default packet size and two windows) is often best.
.LP
When the FIO protocol is enabled (it is a compile time option) it is 
normally necessary to disable it on single lines.
To do this add the string "fN"
to the end of the width entry. E.g:
.I 8fN
It should always be at the end to allow the window and packet fields to
be parsed.
.SH
phone
.LP
The phone number is made up of an optional
alphabetic abbreviation and a numeric part.
The abbreviation is one which appears in the
.I L-dialcodes
file (e.g. london1237654, kent2223456).
.LP
For the DIR devices, this field contains
the name which is matched against the
.I brand
from the devices file and any device which matches (and has a suitable
speed) may be selected.  To make sure that a particular direct line is
used make sure that the
.I brand
only appears on that one line.  It can be usefull to group together
several lines if, for example, all your machines are connected to a local
area network.  In which case it does not matter which one of the
direct lines you select.
.SH
login
.LP
The login information is given as a series of
fields and subfields in the format
.br
.I
:send
.br
?expect
.br
!alt-send
.R
.LP
(This is different from the old format but a program is provided to convert
between the two.)
Strings preceeded by a colon are sent to the remote sytem, so ":uucp\\r"
would send the string "uucp" then a return.
.LP
Strings preceeded by a question mark are searched for on the input from
the line.  In an
.I expect
string '\\?' will match any character.
If it succeeds in finding the specified string it will skip past all subsequent
.I alt-send
or
.I expect
fields to the next
.I send
field or the end of the login script.
.LP
If the string is
.I not
found within fourty-five seconds it will
believe that it has failed.  If the next field is an
.I expect
field it will forget it just failed and start looking for its string.
If it is an 
.I alt-send
string it will send it.  If it is a
.I send
presume that the login has failed and not attempt any further steps
and after some minor cleanups
.I uucico
will exit.
.LP
Strings preceeded by an exclamation mark are
.I only
sent if the preceeding 
.I expect
failed.
.LP
There are several special names available to can be sent
during the login sequence.
The string
.IP EOT 10
will send an EOT character and the string
.IP BREAK
will try to send a BREAK character.
(The
.I BREAK
character is simulated using line speed changes
and null characters and may not work on all
devices and/or systems.)
.IP P_EVEN
from now on all characters sent will have even parity (this is their default)
.IP P_ZERO
switch to zero parity
.IP P_ODD
switch to odd parity
.IP P_ONE
switch to mark parity.
.IP FAILname
give up and call disconnect. This acts similarly to an
.I expect
that has failed except that the logout script (from
.I L.disconnect )
which corresponds to the entry
.I name
is run.
.RE
.LP
A typical entry in the L.sys file would be
.DS L
#
# How to call up west44
west44\ \ Any\ \ ACU\ \ 300\ \ 8\ \ london1237654\ \ \e
	:\er\ \ ?login:\ \ !\er\ \ ?login:\ \ :uucp\er\ \ ?ssword:\ \ :dropdead\er
.DE
.LP
The login script will send a return (to give the remote system a kick)
then wait for the login prompt to appear.  If it doesn't it then sends
another return and waits for the prompt again.  If this time it fails to
find the prompt it gives up attempting to connect.  If it succeeds then it
sends the login name "uucp" and a return. It then waits for the password
prompt before sending the password "dropdead".  If it fails to find the
password prompt it will give up.
.RE
.SH
L.disconnect
.LP
This file is identical in format to the
.I L.sys
file.  The difference is that the only fields looked at are the
.I site
and
.I login
fields.  When disconnecting from a remote system, either due to the
correct completion of a session or due to the string
.I FAIL
being sent in the login script, if there is a entry in this file with that
site name the login script is obeyed.  This can be used to disconnet
from networks, or to shutdown ACU's.
.SH
L.cmds
.LP
This file list all commands that are legal for an incoming
.I uux
command.  Also if the line begins with the string
.I PATH=
it is used to set up the default path of the command.
.LP
For example:
.DS L
	PATH=/bin:/usr/bin:/usr/ucb
	rmail
	rnews
.DE
Is a minimal set of entries.
.PP
Sites running the SYSINFO code will also be able to restrict which
users can export commands with uux using this command.  For example:
.DS L
	PATH=/bin:/usr/bin:/usr/ucb
	rmail	0-20 170
	rnews	0-20 165 189-200
.DE
Will allow only users whose id's are in the range 0-20 or equal to
170 to uux rmail to another site.  This is to restrict the command
to authorise users.  Normally the uid of the mail system daemon will
be provided here and users will therefor have to call the system
mailer to mail off site, thus enforcing the mail system security.
If no uid's are given or if the command is not present in the L.cmds
file then every user is allowed to export the command.
.SH
L.info
.LP
The L.info file is a recently added configuration file primarily used
for the accounting system.  Its format is the same as the L.sys file.
All lines begining with a # are comment lines and are ignored.
.LP
Valid entries in the L.info file are:
.DS L
.SH
debuggers
.LP
Uids entitled to turn on debugging and call asuucp.
It is followed by a whitespace seperated list of uid's or lower-upper
ranges of uid's.
.DS L
	debuggers 0-20 170 188-200
.DE
.SH
uuxqt
.LP
Restricts the grade that uuxqt is prepared to handle:
.DS L
	uuxqt O=A:Wk0800-1800
.DE
Will restrict uuxqt to processing work queued at
grade A only between 0800 and 1800 of weekdays.
The time has the same format as the time field in
an L.sys entry.  Outside this time any grade will
be handled.  Only one O= may be specified, if
several are present then only the first is used.
.SH
exthost
.LP
The name to be given to hosts that are not
specified as local in there L.info entries. This
entry can be overridded when calling uucico with
the -H flag. Without an exthost entry present the
name given will be that of the local machine (ie:
that printed by a call to uuname).
.DS L
	exthost kcl-cs
.DE
.SH
acct
.LP
Wether the accounting system should be enabled or
disabled.  A value of "on" is given is to enable
accounting, "off" (the default) to disable it.
Individual host accounting will not work unless
general accounting is turned on.
.DS L
	acct on
.DE
.SH
host entries.
.LP
A host entry is the name of the host and a series of switches. eg:
.DS L
	west44 local
.DE
Says that the host west44 is a local host, which
means that the local rather that the exthost name
will be given when calling it.
Since the "accton" field is not given accounting
will not be kept for this host.
.DS L
	ukc accton acmail
.DE
Is a non-local host on which full accounting
information is kept and full mail stats are
gathered.
.PP
Hopefully soon a stats gatherer will be available
which will look for charging information from the
L.info entries along the following lines:
.DS L
	kcl-cs local C=in:none C=out:Any:0:0:0:0
	ukc accton acmail C=in:none C=out:Wk0800-1800:1:0:2:100
.DE
Where C specifies a charge entry.  Which is of the
format:
.DS L
	C=in/out:times:per-Kilobyte:per-file:per-sec:per-call
.DE
So for qmc-cs when it calls in there is no charge
(they pay any bills).
If ukc calls between 8am and 6pm any weekday the charge is
1pence/Kbyte, 2pence/sec, 100pence/call.
.RE
.SH
kickthese
.LP
This is the only file for which the free format rules do not apply.
It contains lines each of six characters of the format:
.DS
	ttyXY
.DE
(A five character name ended by a newline).
Which is the name of a device that is going to be used for both login and
dialout.  Devices listed in this file will be forced into the correct mode
by running the
.I KICKINIT
program (see above).
If the device name is just five characters then set the fifth to be dot.
.SH
L.dirs
.LP
In order to minimise the time taken to scan the
.I spool
directory it is split up into multiple subdirectories (but with only one
level of nesting).  All these subdirectories are listed in this file in entries
like the following:
.DS
	C.	336
	D.ukc	336
	D.ukcX	336
	D.	336
	TM.	 72
	X.	336
.DE
When any of the 
.I uucp
programs accesses a file name in 
.I spool
it will match the successive directory names from
.I L.dirs
against the lastpart of the filename.  If the match is successful then the
name is mapped by having the directory name inserted before the lastpart.
For example:
.I /usr/spool/uucp/D.ukcA7890
Using the above 
.I L.dirs
file would be mapped onto
.I /usr/spool/uucp/D.ukc/D.ukcA7890 .
These mapping are invisible to the remote site.
.LP
The first field is the name of an
.I existing
directory of 
.I spool
and the second field is the number of hours a file may be left there before
.I uuclean
may unlink it.
.LP
The best arrangement is to have two extra subdirectories for every site
in the L.sys file:
.RS
.IP C.site 12
Which is for copy commands bound for
.I site .
.IP D.site
Which is for data files bound for 
.I site .
.RE
.LP
.I IMPORTANT :
The filename prefixes are matched in sequential order the first successful
match found is used.  So have the more general names listed after the more
specific ones.  If the ordering in the above example was changed so that
the entry:
.DS
	D.	336
.DE
came first on the list then the name 
.I /usr/spool/uucp/D.ukcA1234
would match it and this would lead to the directory
.I D.ukc
being unused.  This will not produce an error but will increase scanning time
on the queue dramatically.
.NH
Administration
.LP
This section indicates some events and files which must be
administered for the
.I uucp
system.
Some administration can be accomplished by 
.I "shell files"
which can be initiated by
.I crontab
entries.
Others will require manual intervention.
Some sample 
.I "shell files"
are given toward the end of this section.
.SH
SQFILE \- sequence check file
.LP
This file is set up in the
.I program
directory and contains an entry for each remote
system with which you agree to perform conversation
sequence checks.
The initial entry is just the system name of
the remote system.
The first conversation will add two items to the line,
the conversation count, and the date/time of the most
resent conversation.
These items will be updated with each conversation.
If a sequence check fails, the entry will have to
be adjusted.
.SH
TM \- temporary data files
.LP
These files are created in the
.I spool
directory while files are being copied
from a remote machine.
Their names have the form
.IP "" 12
\fBTM\fR.pid.ddd
.LP
where
.I pid
is a process-id and
.I ddd
is a sequential three digit number starting at zero
for each invocation of
.I uucico
and incremented for each file received.
After the entire remote file is received, the
.I TM
file is moved/copied to the requested destination.
If processing is abnormally terminated or the
move/copy fails, the file will remain in the
spool directory.
.LP
The leftover files should be periodically removed;
the
.I uuclean
program is useful in this regard.
The command
.IP "" 12
uuclean\ \ \-A
.LP
will scan the
.I L.dirs
file and remove all files in the specified subdirectory whose age is
greater than the specified hours.
.SH
LOGFILE
.LP
.I LOGFILE
should be removed periodically
since it can grow to be quite substantial proportions since it has an
entry for each transfer where the entry can easily be sixty to a hundred
characters long.
.SH
SYSLOG
.LP
Lists all completed transfers the username and machine that initiated the
transfer the time taken and the direction.
.SH
STST \- system status files
.LP
These files are created in the spool directory by the
.I uucico
program.
They contain information of failures such as login, dialup or
sequence check and will contain a
.I TALKING
status when to machines are conversing.
The form of the file name is
.IP
\fBSTST\fR.sys
.LP
where
.I sys
is the remote system name.
.LP
For ordinary failures (dialup, login), the file will prevent
repeated tries for about one hour.
For sequence check failures, the file must be removed before
any future attempts to converse with that remote system.
.LP
If the file is left due to an aborted run, it may contain a
.I TALKING
status.
In this case, the file must be removed before a conversation
is attempted.
.SH
LCK \- lock files
.LP
Lock files are created for each device in use (e.g. automatic calling
unit) and each system conversing.
This prevents duplicate conversations and multiple attempts to use the
same devices.
The form of the lock file name is
.IP "" 12
\fBLCK..\fRstr
.LP
where
.I str
is either a device or system name.
The files may be left in the spool directory if runs abort.
They will be ignored (reused) after a time of about 24 hours.
When runs abort and calls are desired before the time limit,
the lock files should be removed.
.SH
Shell Files
.LP
The
.I uucp
program will spool work and attempt to start the
.I uucico
program, but the starting of
.I uucico
will sometimes fail.
(No devices available, login failures etc.).
Therefore, the
.I uucico
program should be periodically started.
The command to start
.I uucico
can be put in a ``shell'' file.
The file could contain the commands
.IP
.I program /uulog
.br
.I program /uucico
\ \ \-r1
.LP
Note that the ``\-r1'' option is required to start the
.I uucico
program in
.I MASTER
mode.
.LP
Another shell file may be set up on a daily basis to remove
.I TM ,
.I ST
and
.I LCK
files
and
.I C.
or
.I D.
files for work which can not be accomplished for
reasons like bad phone number, login changes etc.
A shell file containing commands like
.IP
.I program /uuclean
\ \ \-A
.br
.I program /uuclean
\ \ \-pST \-pLCK \-n12
.LP
can be used.
Note the ``\-n12'' option causes the
.I ST
and
.I LCK
files older than 12 hours to be deleted.
The absence of the ``\-n'' option will use a three day time
limit.
.LP
A daily or weekly shell should also be created
to remove or save old
.I LOGFILE s.
A shell like
.IP
cp
.I spool /LOGFILE
\ \ 
.I spool /o.LOGFILE
.br
rm
.I spool /LOGFILE
.LP
can be used.
.SH
Login Entry
.LP
One or more logins should be set up for
.I uucp .
Each of the ``/etc/passwd'' entries should
have the
``\fIprogram\fR/uucico''
as the shell to be executed.
To allow the narrow datawidth code to be used you must also have login
names for
.I uucp4 ,
and
.I uucp7
who have shells of 
``\fIprogram\fR/uucico.4''
and
``\fIprogram\fR/uucico.7''
The login directory is not used, but if the system
has a special directory for use by the users for
sending or receiving files, it should as the
login entry (normally this is
.I /usr/spool/uucppublic
which should be publically read/writeable).
The various logins are used in conjunction with the
.I USERFILE
to restrict file access.
Specifying the
.I shell
argument limits the login to the use of uucp
(
.I uucico )
only.
.SH
File Modes
.LP
It is suggested that the owner and file modes of various
programs and files be set as follows.
.LP
The programs
.I uucp ,
.I uux ,
.I uucico
and
.I uuxqt
should be owned by the
.I uucp
login with the ``setuid'' bit set and only execute
permissions (e.g. mode 04111).
This will prevent outsiders from modifying the programs
to get at a standard
.I shell
for the
.I uucp
logins.
.LP
The
.I L.sys ,
.I SQFILE
and the
.I USERFILE
which are put in the
.I program
directory should be owned by
the
.I uucp
login and set with mode 0400.
.LP
Examples of all the required files and the scripts to be run by cron can be
found in the subdirectories of spool:
.I USR.LIB
and
.I CRON .
.SG MH-1273-DAN-unix
